<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id='sec_represent' xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <title>Representing Points and Motion</title>
<subsection xml:id='ssec_moves' xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <title>A Knight's Moves</title>
        <p>
In chess, the knight is one of the most misunderstood, under-utilized,
and powerful pieces on the board.  All of these traits are due to the
way the knight moves.  Two possible moves for a knight are
                shown in <xref ref='fig_moves'/>.
                If we place <q>Start</q> at <m>(0,0)</m>, the origin of a coordinate system, then
                                <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 is associated with the point <m>(1,2)</m>
                                and
                                <em>Move</em><nbsp/>2 is associated with the point <m>(2,1)</m>.
                                </p>
          <figure xml:id='fig_moves'>
            <caption>Two of a knight's possible moves in chess.</caption>
            <image xml:id='img_tour' width='65%'>
                    <description><p>A grid showing move one as a move from (0,0) to (1,2) and move two as a move from (0,0) to (2,1).</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, every edge/.style={draw,thick,-stealth}]
                            \draw[color=black!15] (-3,-3) grid[step=1] (4,4);
                            \coordinate (start) at (0,0);
                            \coordinate (m1) at (1,2);
                            \coordinate (m2) at (2,1);
                            \fill (start) circle (0.05) node[below] {\emph{Start}};
                            \fill (m1) circle (0.05) node[above] {\emph{Move~1}};
                            \fill (m2) circle (0.05) node[right] {\emph{Move~2}};
                            \draw[dotted] (start) -- (0,2) -- (m1);
                            \draw[dotted] (start) -- (2,0) -- (m2);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id='ssec_vectors'>
        <title>Vectors</title>
        <p>
                        Because <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 and <em>Move</em><nbsp/>2 are motions, it is probably more correct to say that they are associated with moving <em>from</em> <m>(0,0)</m> <em>to</em> <m>(1,2)</m> and <em>from</em> <m>(0,0)</m> <em>to</em> <m>(2,1)</m>, respectively.
        </p>
                                                <p>
                                                        Using <em>vectors</em>, we can represent 
                                                        <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 by the vector <m>\vec{k}_1=\left[\begin{array}{r}+1\\+2\end{array}\right]</m> and <em>Move</em><nbsp/>2 by <m>\vec{k}_2=\left[\begin{array}{r}+2\\+1\end{array}\right]</m>. (We generally don't bother writing <q><m>+</m></q>, though. Instead, a positive value is a motion right or up, and a negative value is left or down.)
          </p>
                                <p>
                                                However, we don't really have an easy way to represent performing <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 twice, or <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 followed by <em>Move</em><nbsp/>2, and so forth. What we can do, though is think of vectors as <em>relative</em> position or <em>motion</em>.
                                                        Thus, we can think of the vector <m>\vec{k}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{r}1\\2\end{array}\right]</m> representing a move<nbsp/><m>1</m><nbsp/>unit to the right and<nbsp/><m>2</m><nbsp/>units up <em>from any point</em>,
                          and similarly, the vector <m>\vec{k}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{r}2\\1\end{array}\right]</m> representing a move<nbsp/><m>2</m><nbsp/>units to the right and<nbsp/><m>1</m><nbsp/>unit up from any point.
          </p>
          <p>
                  Now, we can think about combining the vectors. Starting from <em>Start</em>, <m>(0,0)</m>, we can first perform <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1, <m>\left[\begin{array}{r}1\\2\end{array}\right]</m>, arriving at <m>(1,2)</m>, and then perform <em>Move</em><nbsp/>2, <m>\left[\begin{array}{r}2\\1\end{array}\right]</m>, arriving at <m>(3,3)</m>. We write this combination of moves as 
                  <md>
                          <mrow>\vec{k}_{1}+\vec{k}_{2} &amp;= \left[\begin{array}{r}1\\2\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{r}2\\1\end{array}\right]</mrow>
                          <mrow> &amp;= \left[\begin{array}{r}1+2\\2+1\end{array}\right]</mrow>
                          <mrow> &amp;= \left[\begin{array}{r}3\\3\end{array}\right]</mrow>
                  </md>
          </p>
          <figure xml:id='fig_combining'>
            <caption>Adding Vectors Graphically.</caption>
            <image xml:id='img_combining' width='65%'>
                    <description><p>A visual representation of vector addition.</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, every edge/.style={draw,thick,-stealth}]
                            \draw[color=black!15] (-2,-2) grid[step=1] (5,5);
                            \coordinate (start) at (0,0);
                            \coordinate (k1) at (1,2);
                            \coordinate (k2) at (2,1);
                            \coordinate (k3) at (3,3);
                            \fill (start) circle (0.05);
                            \fill (k1) circle (0.05);
                            \fill (k2) circle (0.05);
                            \fill (k3) circle (0.05);
                            \draw[->] (start) -- node[midway,above left] {\(\vec{k}_{1}\)} (k1);
                            \draw[->] (start) -- node[midway,below right] {\(\vec{k}_{2}\)} (k2);
                            \draw[dashed,->] (k1) -- node[midway,above] {\(\vec{k}_{2}\)} (k3);
                            \draw[dashed,->] (k2) -- node[midway,right] {\(\vec{k}_{1}\)} (k3);
                            \draw[thick,->] (start) -- (k3) node[right] {\(\vec{k}_{1}+\vec{k}_{2}\)};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <p>
                          Graphically, we generally represent vectors as arrows from their starting point, called the <em>tail</em>, to their ending point, called the <em>head</em>. Since vectors represent <em>relative</em> motion, the vector from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(1,2)</m> and the vector from <m>(2,1)</m> to <m>(3,3)</m> are <em>exactly the same vector</em>. Because of this, we can represent adding vectors by placing them head-to-tail, as shown in <xref ref='fig_combining'/> where the <em>resultant</em> vector of the addition <m>\vec{k}_{1}+\vec{k}_{2}</m> is shown with a thick arrow.
          </p>
          <p>
                  <ol>
                          <li>
                                  How do you think you would represent a motion two units right and one unit down, graphically and symbolically?
                          </li>
                          <li>
                                  How do you think you would represent a motion two units left and one unit up, graphically and symbolically?
                          </li>
                          <li>
                                          What vector is the result of performing <em>Move</em><nbsp/>1 twice?
                          </li>
                          <li>
                                          What do you think the notation <m>2\vec{k}_{1}</m> means? 
                          </li>
                  </ol>
          </p>
          </subsection>
          <subsection xml:id='ssec_combining'>
                  <title>Combining Moves</title>
          <p>
An interesting (and rather difficult) set of mathematical problems arise
from considering which squares on different sized chess boards can be
reached by a cohort of several knights.  In this activity we will
consider a slightly different but related idea:  how can we reach
various squares using linear combinations of 
<m>\vec{k}_1</m> and
<m>\vec{k}_2</m>? 
</p>
<p>
        For this activity, you can use the blank grid in <xref ref='fig_grid'/> if you find it helpful. (Place the origin wherever you want!)
<ol>
<li> We know that  
<m>\vec{k}_1+\vec{k}_2</m> allows
you to reach 
<m>(3,3)</m>.  Will any other combination of <m>\vec{k}_{1}</m> and <m>\vec{k}_{2}</m> allow you to reach
the same point?  Explain.

</li>
<li> Is it possible to reach <m>(-1,4)</m> using some combination of <m>\vec{k}_{1}</m> and <m>\vec{k}_{2}</m>?  How (or why not)?
</li>
<li> Is it possible to reach <m>(-1,-1)</m>?  How (or why not)?
</li>
                          <li xml:id='q_neg_one'>
                                          What do you think the notation <m>-1\vec{k}_{1}</m> means? 
                          </li>
                          <li xml:id='q_neg_two'>
                                          What do you think the notation <m>-2\vec{k}_{2}</m> means? 
                          </li>
                          <li xml:id='q_minus_two'>
                                  What do you think the notation <m>\vec{k}_{1}-2\vec{k}_{2}</m> means? 
                          </li>
                          <li> Would your responses to any of the above questions change in light of your answers to <xref ref='q_neg_one'/>, <xref ref='q_neg_two'/>, and <xref ref='q_minus_two'/>?
</li>
<li> Would your responses to any of the above questions change if you
could Move<nbsp/>a fractional part of 
<m>\vec{k}_1</m> and 
<m>\vec{k}_2</m>?  (Say <m>\frac{1}{3}\vec{k}_{1}</m>, for example.)
</li>
<li> If any real number multiples of 
<m>\vec{k}_1</m> and 
<m>\vec{k}_2</m>
are allowed, can you reach the point <m>(1,0)</m>?  Explain.
</li>
<li> If any real number multiples of 
<m>\vec{k}_1</m> and 
<m>\vec{k}_2</m>
are allowed, can you reach the point <m>(0,1)</m>?  Explain.
</li>
<li> If any real number multiples of 
<m>\vec{k}_1</m> and 
<m>\vec{k}_2</m>
are allowed, which squares can you reach?  Explain.
</li>
<li>Now let's do away with the idea that we have to land on a corner of a square and think about points in the plane more generally. How could you reach the point <m>\left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{4}\right)</m> using a combination of <m>\vec{k}_{1}</m> and <m>\vec{k}_{2}</m>?
</li>
<li>More generally, what is the process to reach a given a point <m>(a,b)</m> in the plane using combinations of <m>\vec{k}_1</m> and <m>\vec{k}_2</m>?
</li>
</ol>
        </p>
          <figure xml:id='fig_grid'>
            <caption>A Blank Grid.</caption>
            <image xml:id='img_grid' width='100%'>
                    <description><p>A blank grid.</p></description>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, every edge/.style={draw,thick,-stealth}]
                            \draw[color=black!35] (-10,-10) grid[step=1] (10,10);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>

</subsection>
</section>
